+  `input_ff_upload` ← `evdev_ioctl`. O alvo é um dispositivo criado por `/dev/uinput` e o kernel
+  espera, sem limite de tempo e sem poder ser interrompido, que o processo dono desse dispositivo
+  responda ao pedido.
+- A outra thread, de vídeo, continua trocando o quadro do framebuffer (`FBIOPAN_DISPLAY`, ~20 por
+  segundo): por isso a tela fica no "loading" e a CPU aparece alta.
+- Antes disso, a thread principal passa breves momentos em D em `evdev_release`/`joydev_release`
+  (`synchronize_rcu`): é o fechamento normal de dispositivos de entrada no início, não o problema.
+- **Defeito da própria sonda:** o log diz 240 s, mas cobre ~15 s de relógio. O `sleep` do shell do
+  aparelho voltava na hora (provável efeito do `sync` em segundo plano terminando durante a espera).
+  Corrigido na versão 2 (8.5). A conclusão acima não depende disso, mas o log não mostra se o travamento
+  teria passado depois.
+
+### 8.3 Causa provável (análise estática, não confirmada no aparelho)
+
+O daemon de entrada do JukaMix para o TSP/TSP-S (`System/resources/tsp_inputd`, 41 144 bytes, o mesmo
+arquivo que `tsps_inputd`) cria o controle virtual "Xbox 360" na função `trimui_setup_xpad` **anunciando
+force feedback**: liga `EV_FF`, seis bits `FF_*` (`0x50 0x60 0x51 0x58 0x59 0x5a`) e define
+`ff_effects_max = 16`. Mas o daemon **nunca atende** esses pedidos: não há `UI_BEGIN_FF_UPLOAD` /
+`UI_END_FF_UPLOAD` (0xc06055c8/c9) em nenhum dos três binários, e as três chamadas `read()` do programa
+leem só o inotify e a porta serial, nunca o descritor do uinput. O SDL2 (usado pelo RetroArch) envia um
+efeito de vibração assim que abre um controle com FF; com esse dispositivo o pedido nunca termina.
+
+- Esse recurso entrou no upstream em 2025-11-26 (commit `a984db64`, "Fix Pro S controls") e continua em
+  `b27bd415` e `321a16cd`. O `tsp_inputd` de 2025-09-01 (`e8804701`, 26 912 bytes, também o padrão em
+  `trimui/app/trimui_inputd`) **não** tem FF.
+- **Hipótese para "antes funcionava":** o cartão novo começa com o daemon padrão (sem FF); o
+  `inputd_switcher` instala o do dispositivo (com FF), mas só passa a valer depois de um reinício
+  (`restart_inputd` não reinicia com o MainUI ativo). Isso explicaria jogos abrindo na primeira sessão e
+  o travamento depois. É uma hipótese: o relatório não mostra qual daemon estava instalado.
+- **Não confirmado:** que o dono do dispositivo é o `trimui_inputd` (o log não listava quem segura
+  `/dev/uinput`; a sonda v2 passa a listar); e por que outros usuários do JukaMix não relatam o mesmo
+  (não investigado; pode ser que relatem).
+
+### 8.4 Correção entregue (`RetroArch-fix-sdcard.zip`)
+
+`scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py` troca **8 instruções de 32 bits** em `trimui_setup_xpad`: as sete
+chamadas `ioctl` que ligam `EV_FF` e os `FF_*` viram `nop` (os retornos nunca eram checados) e
+`ff_effects_max` passa de 16 para 0. O resto do binário é idêntico (41 144 bytes, mesmos offsets),
+então todas as correções de entrada mais novas do daemon continuam. O script confere as instruções ao
+redor antes de escrever e se recusa a mexer em qualquer outro arquivo.
+
+| Arquivo no cartão | Antes | Depois |
+|---|---|---|
+| `trimui/app/trimui_inputd` (daemon em uso) | conforme instalado pelo switcher (provavelmente o de 41 144 B, crc32 `5055f537`) ou o padrão de 26 912 B (`3356097d`) | 41 144 B, crc32 `1802bfaf` |
+| `System/resources/tsp_inputd` e `tsps_inputd` (origem do switcher) | 41 144 B, crc32 `5055f537` | 41 144 B, crc32 `1802bfaf` |
+
+Os três ficam iguais, então o `inputd_switcher` (que compara o instalado com o de `System/resources`)
+não desfaz a correção. O `brickpro_inputd` não tem FF e não foi tocado. Teste:
+`bash tests/inputd_noff_test.sh` (24 checagens: exatamente 8 palavras mudam, nos offsets esperados;
+o disassembly da função não tem mais `ioctl` de FF; os arquivos do cartão são idênticos).
+
+**Limite desta correção:** ela foi verificada por comparação de bytes e disassembly; o binário é
+aarch64 e depende de `/dev/uinput` e da UART do aparelho, por isso não foi executado aqui.
+
+**Se não resolver:** a sonda v2 diz exatamente o que acontece (8.5). Alternativa já disponível no
+próprio release do JukaMix: copiar o `trimui/app/trimui_inputd` original (26 912 B) por cima do daemon
+do cartão e do `System/resources/tsp_inputd`; ele não tem FF, mas também não tem as correções de
+entrada de 26–28/11/2025 (controles do Pro S, D-pad e detecção de dispositivo).
+
+### 8.5 Sonda v2 (`ra_probe.sh`)
+
+- Espera por **tempo real** (`/proc/uptime`), repetindo o `sleep` se ele voltar antes; cada amostra
+  mostra `t=+Ns` real e o `uptime`. A duração de 240 s passa a ser real.
+- Quando a thread principal está em `EVIOCSFF`, registra uma única vez: o descritor e o nó
+  `/dev/input/eventN`, o nome e as capacidades (`B: FF=`) desse dispositivo, a lista dos dispositivos de
+  entrada, **quais processos seguram `/dev/uinput`** e o tamanho/crc32 do daemon instalado e dos de
+  `System/resources`.
+- Continua só lendo `/proc` e nunca interfere no RetroArch. Teste: `bash tests/ra_probe_test.sh`
+  (44 checagens).
+
+### 8.6 Como conferir no aparelho
+
+1. Extrair `RetroArch-fix-sdcard.zip` na raiz do cartão (aceitar substituir) e **desligar e ligar de
+   novo** (reinício completo).
+2. Abrir Apps → RetroArch (e depois um jogo por um lançador de emulador). Esperado: o menu abre.
+3. Qualquer que seja o resultado, `System/logs/ra_probe.log` (ou *System Logs - Collect*) mostra o
+   que aconteceu. A linha `installed input daemon: 41144 bytes crc32=1802bfaf` confirma que o daemon
+   corrigido está em uso; `5055f537` significa que o original continua instalado.
diff --git a/scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py b/scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..3474ff53
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py
@@ -0,0 +1,184 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env python3
+"""Stop a JukaMix trimui_inputd (aarch64) from advertising force feedback.
+
+Why
+---
+The tsp/tsps input daemons built since 2025-11-26 create the virtual Xbox-360 pad with
+EV_FF, six FF_* bits and ff_effects_max=16 (function trimui_setup_xpad), but nothing in
+the daemon ever reads the uinput fd or answers UI_BEGIN_FF_UPLOAD / UI_END_FF_UPLOAD.
+Any program that uploads a rumble effect to that pad (SDL2 does it when it opens the pad,
+and RetroArch's SDL2 joypad driver does that at start-up) then sleeps for ever in
+uinput_request_submit(), state D, and cannot be killed.  The effect: Apps > RetroArch (and
+every game launched through it) stays on the loading screen.
+
+What it changes
+---------------
+Eight 32-bit instructions inside trimui_setup_xpad:
+  * the seven `bl ioctl` calls that do UI_SET_EVBIT(EV_FF) and UI_SET_FFBIT(...) x6
+    become `nop`  (their return values are never checked);
+  * `mov w0,#16` (uinput_user_dev.ff_effects_max) becomes `mov w0,#0`.
+Nothing else in the file changes, so every newer input fix in the daemon is kept.
+
+Usage
+-----
+  inputd-disable-ff.py --check FILE            prints original | patched | unknown
+  inputd-disable-ff.py FILE -o OUT             writes the patched copy (FILE is not touched)
+  inputd-disable-ff.py FILE --in-place         patches FILE (a .orig copy is kept)
+
+Exit status: 0 on success (also when already patched), 1 when the file is not the build
+this script knows how to patch (nothing is written in that case).
+"""
+import argparse
+import hashlib
+import shutil
+import struct
+import sys
+
+NOP = 0xD503201F
+MOV_W0_0 = 0x52800000          # mov w0, #0
+MOV_W0_16 = 0x52800200         # mov w0, #0x10
+IOCTL_PLT = 0x4011A0           # ioctl@plt in the 41144-byte tsp/tsps build
+
+# Each call site: (address of the `bl ioctl`, address of `mov w2,#code`, w2 value,
+#                  address of `mov x1,#request` (low half; the high half 0x4004 follows)).
+#   UI_SET_EVBIT = 0x40045564 (w2 = EV_FF = 0x15), UI_SET_FFBIT = 0x4004556b (w2 = FF_*).
+CALL_SITES = (
+    (0x403C4C, 0x403C38, 0x15, 0x403C3C, 0x5564),
+    (0x403C60, 0x403C50, 0x50, 0x403C54, 0x556B),
+    (0x403C74, 0x403C64, 0x60, 0x403C68, 0x556B),
+    (0x403C88, 0x403C78, 0x51, 0x403C7C, 0x556B),
+    (0x403C9C, 0x403C8C, 0x58, 0x403C90, 0x556B),
+    (0x403CB0, 0x403CA0, 0x59, 0x403CA4, 0x556B),
+    (0x403CC4, 0x403CB4, 0x5A, 0x403CB8, 0x556B),
+)
+MOV_SITE = 0x403CC8            # mov w0,#16  (uinput_user_dev.ff_effects_max)
+
+
+def mov_w2(imm):
+    return 0x52800000 | (imm << 5) | 2
+
+
+def mov_x1(imm):
+    return 0xD2800000 | (imm << 5) | 1
+
+
+def load_segments(data):
+    if data[:4] != b"\x7fELF" or data[4] != 2 or data[5] != 1:
+        raise ValueError("not a 64-bit little-endian ELF file")
+    if struct.unpack_from("<H", data, 0x12)[0] != 0xB7:
+        raise ValueError("not an aarch64 ELF file")
+    phoff = struct.unpack_from("<Q", data, 0x20)[0]
+    phentsize, phnum = struct.unpack_from("<HH", data, 0x36)
+    segs = []
+    for i in range(phnum):
+        ptype, _flags, off, va, _pa, filesz, _memsz, _align = struct.unpack_from(
+            "<IIQQQQQQ", data, phoff + i * phentsize)
+        if ptype == 1:
+            segs.append((va, off, filesz))
+    return segs
+
+
+def file_offset(segs, va):
+    for seg_va, off, size in segs:
+        if seg_va <= va < seg_va + size:
+            return off + va - seg_va
+    raise ValueError("address %#x is not in a loadable segment" % va)
+
+
+def word(data, segs, va):
+    return struct.unpack_from("<I", data, file_offset(segs, va))[0]
+
+
+def bl_target(w, va):
+    if (w >> 26) != 0b100101:
+        return None
+    imm = w & 0x3FFFFFF
+    if imm & (1 << 25):
+        imm -= 1 << 26
+    return va + imm * 4
+
+
+def state(data):
+    """original | patched | unknown for the bytes of this file."""
+    try:
+        segs = load_segments(data)
+        calls = nops = 0
+        for bl_va, w2_va, w2_imm, x1_va, x1_imm in CALL_SITES:
+            # the surrounding instructions must be exactly the ones we expect, otherwise
+            # this is another build and the offsets mean nothing
+            if word(data, segs, w2_va) != mov_w2(w2_imm):
+                return "unknown"
+            if word(data, segs, x1_va) != mov_x1(x1_imm):
+                return "unknown"
+            w = word(data, segs, bl_va)
+            if bl_target(w, bl_va) == IOCTL_PLT:
+                calls += 1
+            elif w == NOP:
+                nops += 1
+            else:
+                return "unknown"
+        mov = word(data, segs, MOV_SITE)
+        if calls == len(CALL_SITES) and mov == MOV_W0_16:
+            return "original"
+        if nops == len(CALL_SITES) and mov == MOV_W0_0:
+            return "patched"
+        return "unknown"
+    except (ValueError, struct.error):
+        return "unknown"
+
+
+def patch(data):
+    segs = load_segments(data)
+    out = bytearray(data)
+    for bl_va, *_rest in CALL_SITES:
+        struct.pack_into("<I", out, file_offset(segs, bl_va), NOP)
+    struct.pack_into("<I", out, file_offset(segs, MOV_SITE), MOV_W0_0)
+    return bytes(out)
+
+
+def sha(data):
+    return hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()
+
+
+def main(argv=None):
+    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
+    ap.add_argument("file")
+    ap.add_argument("-o", "--output")
+    ap.add_argument("--in-place", action="store_true")
+    ap.add_argument("--check", action="store_true")
+    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
+
+    with open(args.file, "rb") as fh:
+        data = fh.read()
+    st = state(data)
+
+    if args.check:
+        print(st)
+        return 0 if st in ("original", "patched") else 1
+
+    if st == "unknown":
+        print("%s: not the tsp/tsps build this script knows (nothing written)" % args.file,
+              file=sys.stderr)
+        return 1
+
+    new = patch(data) if st == "original" else data
+    if args.in_place:
+        if st == "original":
+            shutil.copy2(args.file, args.file + ".orig")
+            with open(args.file, "wb") as fh:
+                fh.write(new)
+        target = args.file
+    elif args.output:
+        with open(args.output, "wb") as fh:
+            fh.write(new)
+        target = args.output
+    else:
+        ap.error("give -o OUT or --in-place")
+
+    print("%s: %s -> patched, %d bytes, sha256 %s" % (
+        target, st, len(new), sha(new)))
+    return 0
+
+
+if __name__ == "__main__":
+    sys.exit(main())
diff --git a/tests/inputd_noff_test.sh b/tests/inputd_noff_test.sh
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..262c4eed
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/inputd_noff_test.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,109 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Off-device checks for the tsp/tsps input daemon that no longer advertises force feedback
+# (scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py and the daemons shipped on the card).
+# Usage: bash tests/inputd_noff_test.sh     Needs: python3. Optional: llvm-objdump, git, sha256sum.
+#
+# The daemon cannot be executed here (aarch64 build, needs /dev/uinput and the UART), so the
+# checks are structural: which bytes change, what the disassembly of the patched function
+# looks like, and that the files on the card are the patched build.
+
+ROOT=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)
+PATCHER=$ROOT/scripts/inputd-disable-ff.py
+T=$(mktemp -d)
+trap 'rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
+
+pass=0
+fail=0
+ok() { pass=$((pass + 1)); printf 'ok   %s\n' "$1"; }
+bad() { fail=$((fail + 1)); printf 'FAIL %s\n' "$1"; }
+eq() { if [ "$2" = "$3" ]; then ok "$1"; else bad "$1  (got '$2', want '$3')"; fi; }
+
+command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
+    echo "python3 not found"
+    exit 1
+}
+
+# The pristine upstream build (JukaMix d5102fab) is the reference for the byte diff.  Inside a
+# clone it comes from git; otherwise the tests that need it are skipped.
+UP=$T/upstream_tsp_inputd
+have_up=0
+if git -C "$ROOT" show d5102fab:System/resources/tsp_inputd >"$UP" 2>/dev/null && [ -s "$UP" ]; then
+    have_up=1
+fi
+
+# ---- 1. what the script recognises -------------------------------------------------------
+if [ "$have_up" = 1 ]; then
+    eq "upstream tsp build is recognised as original" "$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$UP")" original
+    eq "upstream tsp build size" "$(wc -c <"$UP" | tr -d ' ')" 41144
+fi
+python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$ROOT/trimui/app/trimui_inputd" >/dev/null 2>&1
+st=$?
+# either the patched tsp build (this card) or the old 26912-byte build: never "original"
+out=$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$ROOT/trimui/app/trimui_inputd" 2>&1)
+case "$out" in
+    patched | unknown) ok "default daemon is not the unpatched tsp build ($out, status $st)" ;;
+    *) bad "default daemon is the unpatched tsp build" ;;
+esac
+head -c 4096 /dev/urandom >"$T/junk.bin"
+eq "random bytes are refused" "$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$T/junk.bin" 2>/dev/null)" unknown
+if python3 -I "$PATCHER" "$T/junk.bin" -o "$T/junk.out" 2>/dev/null; then bad "patcher accepted a foreign file"; else ok "patcher refuses a foreign file"; fi
+[ -e "$T/junk.out" ] && bad "patcher wrote output for a foreign file" || ok "nothing written for a foreign file"
+
+# ---- 2. the patch itself ------------------------------------------------------------------
+if [ "$have_up" = 1 ]; then
+    python3 -I "$PATCHER" "$UP" -o "$T/patched.bin" >/dev/null
+    eq "patched copy is recognised" "$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$T/patched.bin")" patched
+    eq "size is unchanged" "$(wc -c <"$T/patched.bin" | tr -d ' ')" "$(wc -c <"$UP" | tr -d ' ')"
+    python3 -I "$PATCHER" "$T/patched.bin" -o "$T/twice.bin" >/dev/null
+    cmp -s "$T/patched.bin" "$T/twice.bin" && ok "patching twice changes nothing" || bad "patching twice changed the file"
+    changed=$(python3 -I - "$UP" "$T/patched.bin" <<'PY'
+import sys
+a = open(sys.argv[1], 'rb').read()
+b = open(sys.argv[2], 'rb').read()
+words = sorted({i // 4 * 4 for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]})
+print(len(words), ' '.join('%x' % w for w in words))
+PY
+)
+    eq "exactly eight 32-bit words differ, at the expected offsets" "$changed" "8 3c4c 3c60 3c74 3c88 3c9c 3cb0 3cc4 3cc8"
+    [ "$(cmp -l "$UP" "$T/patched.bin" | awk '$1 < 15437 || $1 > 15564' | wc -l)" = 0 ] \
+        && ok "no byte outside the 8 patched words changes (file offsets 0x3c4c-0x3ccb)" || bad "bytes changed outside the function"
+    cp "$UP" "$T/inplace.bin"
+    python3 -I "$PATCHER" "$T/inplace.bin" --in-place >/dev/null
+    cmp -s "$T/inplace.bin.orig" "$UP" && ok "--in-place keeps the original as .orig" || bad "--in-place lost the original"
+    cmp -s "$T/inplace.bin" "$T/patched.bin" && ok "--in-place result equals -o result" || bad "--in-place differs from -o"
+fi
+
+# ---- 3. disassembly of the patched function ------------------------------------------------
+SRC=$ROOT/System/resources/tsp_inputd
+if command -v llvm-objdump >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+    dis=$(llvm-objdump -d --no-show-raw-insn "$SRC" 2>/dev/null | sed -n '/^ *403c38:/,/^ *403cf0:/p')
+    n_nop=$(printf '%s\n' "$dis" | grep -cE '^ *403c(4c|60|74|88|9c|b0|c4):\s+nop')
+    eq "the seven FF ioctl calls are nops" "$n_nop" 7
+    printf '%s\n' "$dis" | grep -qE '^ *403cc8:\s+mov\s+w0, #0x0\b' && ok "ff_effects_max is set to 0" || bad "ff_effects_max is not 0"
+    # what must survive: the UI_DEV_SETUP (0x405c5503) and UI_DEV_CREATE (0x5501) calls
+    printf '%s\n' "$dis" | grep -qE '#0x5503' && printf '%s\n' "$dis" | grep -qE '#0x5501' \
+        && ok "UI_DEV_SETUP and UI_DEV_CREATE are still issued" || bad "device creation calls are missing"
+    # no ioctl at all between the EV_FF setup and UI_DEV_SETUP any more
+    between=$(printf '%s\n' "$dis" | sed -n '/^ *403c38:/,/^ *403cdc:/p' | grep -c 'bl.*ioctl')
+    eq "no ioctl left between the FF setup and UI_DEV_SETUP" "$between" 0
+    # nothing else in the whole daemon uploads or sets FF bits any more
+    ffbits=$(llvm-objdump -d --no-show-raw-insn "$SRC" 2>/dev/null | grep -A3 '#0x556b' | grep -c 'bl.*ioctl')
+    eq "no UI_SET_FFBIT request is followed by an ioctl anywhere" "$ffbits" 0
+else
+    echo "skip disassembly checks (llvm-objdump not found)"
+fi
+
+# ---- 4. what is shipped on the card ---------------------------------------------------------
+eq "tsp_inputd on the card is patched" "$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$SRC")" patched
+eq "tsps_inputd on the card is patched" "$(python3 -I "$PATCHER" --check "$ROOT/System/resources/tsps_inputd")" patched
+cmp -s "$SRC" "$ROOT/System/resources/tsps_inputd" && ok "tsp and tsps builds are the same file" || bad "tsp and tsps builds differ"
+cmp -s "$SRC" "$ROOT/trimui/app/trimui_inputd" && ok "the installed default daemon equals the patched tsp build" || bad "trimui/app/trimui_inputd is not the patched tsp build"
+if [ "$have_up" = 1 ]; then
+    # same function, same size, different bytes: the card must not still carry the original
+    cmp -s "$SRC" "$UP" && bad "tsp_inputd on the card is still the upstream build" || ok "tsp_inputd on the card differs from upstream"
+fi
+python3 -I -m py_compile "$PATCHER" 2>/dev/null && ok "patcher compiles" || bad "patcher does not compile"
+
+echo
+echo "$pass passed, $fail failed"
+[ "$fail" -eq 0 ]
diff --git a/tests/ra_probe_test.sh b/tests/ra_probe_test.sh
index d6894b59..caaa9edb 100755
--- a/tests/ra_probe_test.sh
+++ b/tests/ra_probe_test.sh
@@ -127,6 +127,75 @@ JUKAMIX_PROC=$P JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_SECONDS=17 JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_FIND_SECONDS=2 JUKA
 has "flag: strace requested by System/etc/ra_strace" "$LOG" 'strace: attaching'
 rm -f "$T/sd/System/etc/ra_strace"
 
+# ---- 6b. main thread blocked in a force-feedback upload (the field failure) -------------
+# aarch64 syscall 29 = ioctl, 0x40304580 = EVIOCSFF, fd 12 = a uinput-backed pad
+sed -i 's/ S 1 1 1/ D 1 1 1/' "$P/4321/task/4321/stat"
+printf 'uinput_request_submit.part.1' >"$P/4321/wchan"
+printf '29 0xc 0x40304580 0xb3761d0 0x435 0xb376200 0x0 0x7fec367830 0x7f9a26bd90\n' >"$P/4321/syscall"
+ln -s /dev/input/event3 "$P/4321/fd/12"
+ln -s /dev/input/event0 "$P/4321/fd/11"
+mkdir -p "$P/bus/input" "$P/2272/fd" "$T/sd/trimui/app" "$T/sd/System/resources"
+cat >"$P/bus/input/devices" <<'EOF'
+I: Bus=0019 Vendor=0001 Product=0001 Version=0100
+N: Name="axp2202-pek"
+H: Handlers=kbd event0
+B: EV=3
+
+I: Bus=0003 Vendor=045e Product=028e Version=0110
+N: Name="Microsoft X-Box 360 pad"
+P: Phys=
+H: Handlers=js0 event3
+B: EV=20000b
+B: FF=100040000 0
+EOF
+ln -s /dev/uinput "$P/2272/fd/5"
+printf 'trimui_inputd\n' >"$P/2272/comm"
+ln -s /mnt/SDCARD/trimui/app/trimui_inputd "$P/2272/exe"
+head -c 41144 /dev/zero >"$T/sd/trimui/app/trimui_inputd"
+head -c 26912 /dev/zero >"$T/sd/System/resources/tsp_inputd"
+: >"$LOG"
+JUKAMIX_PROC=$P JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_SECONDS=6 JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_FIND_SECONDS=2 JUKAMIX_STRACE=$T/fake_strace \
+    JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_INTERVAL=2 sh "$PROBE" "$BIN"
+has "FF: says the thread waits in the uinput request" "$LOG" 'wchan=uinput_request_submit'
+has "FF: recognises EVIOCSFF and the fd" "$LOG" 'blocked in EVIOCSFF .* on fd 12'
+has "FF: resolves the fd to the event node" "$LOG" 'fd 12 -> /dev/input/event3'
+has "FF: names the pad behind that node" "$LOG" 'N: Name="Microsoft X-Box 360 pad"'
+has "FF: shows the pad advertises force feedback" "$LOG" 'B: FF=100040000'
+hasnt "FF: leaves out the unrelated device block" "$LOG" 'H: Handlers=kbd event0'
+has "FF: lists who holds /dev/uinput" "$LOG" 'pid 2272 \(trimui_inputd\) fd 5'
+has "FF: reports the installed daemon size" "$LOG" 'installed input daemon: 41144 bytes'
+has "FF: reports the shipped daemon size" "$LOG" 'resources/tsp_inputd: 26912 bytes'
+n=$(grep -c 'blocked in EVIOCSFF' "$LOG")
+if [ "$n" = 1 ]; then ok "FF: the input context is logged once, not every sample"; else bad "FF: input context logged $n times"; fi
+sed -i 's/ D 1 1 1/ S 1 1 1/' "$P/4321/task/4321/stat"
+printf 'io_schedule' >"$P/4321/wchan"
+printf '0 0x3 0x0\n' >"$P/4321/syscall"
+
+# ---- 6c. real time: a sleep that returns early must not shorten the probe ---------------
+# stub sleep: every odd call returns at once (the busybox-ash behaviour seen on the device),
+# every even call "sleeps" one second by moving the fake uptime forward
+mkdir -p "$T/bin"
+printf '100.00 200.00\n' >"$P/uptime"
+: >"$T/sleep_calls"
+cat >"$T/bin/sleep" <<EOF2
+#!/bin/sh
+echo "\$1" >>"$T/sleep_calls"
+n=\$(wc -l <"$T/sleep_calls")
+if [ \$((n % 2)) -eq 0 ]; then
+    read -r up rest <"$P/uptime"
+    printf '%s.00 %s\n' \$((\${up%%.*} + 1)) "\$rest" >"$P/uptime"
+fi
+EOF2
+chmod +x "$T/bin/sleep"
+: >"$LOG"
+PATH=$T/bin:$PATH JUKAMIX_PROC=$P JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_SECONDS=6 JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_FIND_SECONDS=2 \
+    JUKAMIX_STRACE=$T/none JUKAMIX_RA_PROBE_INTERVAL=3 sh "$PROBE" "$BIN"
+has "time: first sample at +0 s" "$LOG" '^-- t=\+0s .*uptime=100'
+has "time: second sample after 3 real seconds" "$LOG" '^-- t=\+3s .*uptime=103'
+has "time: ends after 6 real seconds" "$LOG" 'probe finished after 6s'
+calls=$(wc -l <"$T/sleep_calls")
+if [ "$calls" -ge 12 ]; then ok "time: retried the early-returning sleep ($calls calls for 6 s)"; else bad "time: only $calls sleep calls"; fi
+
 # ---- 7. the launcher hooks the probe in and stays valid POSIX sh --------------------------
 grep -q 'ra_probe.sh' "$ROOT/Apps/RetroArch/launch.sh" && ok "launch.sh starts the probe" || bad "launch.sh does not start the probe"
 last=$(tail -n 1 "$ROOT/Apps/RetroArch/launch.sh")
